  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- A surge of upper level moisture into the region is causing cloud\n  cover, virga, and increasing humidity across the region today. \n\n- Thunderstorms this evening are forecast for southwest Montana\n  this afternoon including southern Powell, Granite, Deer Lodge,\n  and Silver Bow counties. \n\n- Thunderstorms are in the forecast through the week for much of\n  western Montana and portions of central Idaho. Storms will\n  continue in southwest Montana through the weekend. \n\nUpper level moisture continues to stream into the region from the\nsouthwest. This is causing cloud cover across much of the region,\nand virga, which in turn moistens the lower elevations. As a\nresult, we've seen quite an increase in humidity with dew points \nincreasing into the upper 40s and low 50s across the region. High\nresolution, short term ensembles show thunderstorms this \nafternoon mainly affecting southern Powell, Deer Lodge, Granite, \nand Silver Bow counties in western Montana. There is a marginal \nrisk for these storms to cause wind gusts near 60 mph, but the \nhigher the dew points get this evening, the less likely that \nbecomes. \n\nThe trend continues tomorrow with precipitable water, which is a\nmeasure of how much water vapor is available to condense and fall\nas rain, rising up to around 1.5 inches. This is around 200 to \n250 percent of average for this time of year. This would be very \nalarming, except that when there's that much moisture it usually \nclouds up and prevents enough instability from building up for \nstrong storms. Currently ensembles are forecasting light to \nmoderate rain showers embedded in the larger cloud shield across \nthe region. \n\nThis general pattern persists into next week. The details will\ndetermine which areas see thunderstorms each day, with southwest\nMontana being the most consistent area. But overall, the general\nsouthwest flow pattern with thunderstorms remains through early\nnext week.",
